wed as an m -dimensional “cube” with ∏\nm\ni = 1\ndi cells (positions). A cell\nx = (x 1, · · · , xm) ∈ S\nm\nrepresents a number of objects (given by function f req ()) with all m attribute\nvalues equal to (x 1, · · · , xm). We de fi ne the HIERDENC hyper-cube C (x0, r) ⊂ S\nm\n, centered at cell\nx0 with radius r, as follows:\nC (x0, r) = {x : x ∈ S\nm\nand dist (x, x0) ≤ r and f req (x) > 0}.\nThe dist (·) is a distance function, which may be the Hamming distance.\nFigure 12.1 illustrates two HIERDENC hyper-cubes in a 3-dimensional cube. Since r =1, the\nhyper-cubes are visualized as “crosses” in 3D and are not shown as actually having a cubic shape. A\ncube’s cells for which f req () is 0 do not represent any objects in the dataset. Normally, a hyper-cube\nwill equal a subspace of S\nm\n.\nThe density of a subspace X ⊂ S\nm\n, where X could equal a hyper-cube C (x0, r) ⊂ S\nm\n, involves\nthe sum of f req () evaluated over all cells of X:\ndensity (X) = ∑\nc ∈ X\nf req (c)"],[35518,"H0 :\nm m E C =\nH a E C:\nm m ≤\nUsing the Data Analysis feature of the Tools menu in Excel, the Descriptive Statistics selec-\ntion generates the data provided in Tables 18.1 to 18.3 (after some editing efforts such as\nconverting decimals into percentiles, etc.):\nTable 18.1 Edited version of the American cities database.\nc ity r egion c hange in\nd ept s tore\ns ales\nUnempl\nr ate\nc hange in\nn onfarm\ne mpl\nc hange\nin i ncome\nFactory\nw orkers\nc hange\nin Factory\nw orkers\ni ncome\nc hange in\nc onstruction\na ctivity"],[35466,"H0 0:\nm m =\nHa:\nm m ≠ 0\nIn such cases, we will be convinced that H\n0\nis false if the test statistic X is far enough away\nfrom m\n0\n. There are two critical values, X 1\n∗\nand X 2\n∗\n, again chosen to control a, and generally\nequidistant from m. Because of this symmetry,\nP H and P H ( | ) ( | ) X X X X < = > = 1 0 2 0\n2 2\n∗ ∗\na\na\nDerivations identical to those for the two one-tail tests give us formulas for the critical values:\nX